```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — R1 losing its OSPF neighbor toward CSW1 and all
internal traffic falling back to the CSW2 path — is real, but the stated
cause (physical cable failure on the CSW1 eth0 ↔ R1 eth0 segment) is
incorrect. Both interfaces show LOWER_UP / state UP with active TX/RX byte
counts, confirming the cable is intact and L1/L2 is healthy.

The actual fault is an OSPF passive-interface misconfiguration introduced on
CSW1 during the "routine config change." CSW1's running configuration
contains `ip ospf passive` under `interface eth0`. In FRR/OSPF, a passive
interface is still advertised into the OSPF topology but suppresses all OSPF
hello transmission and reception on that interface. As a result, no adjacency
can form: `show ip ospf interface eth0` on CSW1 explicitly reports "No Hellos
(Passive interface)" and "Neighbor Count is 0." R1 therefore has no OSPF
neighbor via eth0 (10.0.0.33/30) and its full routing table shows every
internal prefix resolving exclusively via 10.0.0.38 (CSW2, eth1).

CSW1's peering links to the distribution switches (eth3–eth6, bond0) are
unaffected and remain in Full/DR state; only the WAN-facing eth0 has the
passive flag.

Root cause: `ip ospf passive` was accidentally applied to CSW1 `interface
eth0` during the change window, silencing OSPF hellos on the CSW1–R1 link.

Corrective fix: On CSW1, enter vtysh and remove the flag:
  `configure terminal → interface eth0 → no ip ospf passive → end → write
  memory`. The OSPF adjacency between CSW1 (10.0.0.77) and R1 (10.0.0.76)
  will re-establish within one dead interval (~40 s), restoring dual-path
  routing. No physical recabling is needed.
=== END ===
